# Cursor Rules - Think Together Project

## Modal/Dialog Implementation Standards

When creating modals or dialogs using the `Dialog` component from `@/shared/ui/dialog`, follow these standards:

### Layout Structure

1. **Use Flexbox Layout**: Always use `flex flex-col` on `DialogContent` to create a proper vertical layout
   ```tsx
   <DialogContent className="max-w-[calc(100%-2rem)] sm:max-w-4xl max-h-[90vh] flex flex-col p-0">
   ```

2. **Fixed Header**: Make `DialogHeader` fixed with `flex-shrink-0` and custom padding
   ```tsx
   <DialogHeader className="flex-shrink-0 px-6 pt-6 pb-4">
   ```

3. **Scrollable Content Area**: Wrap the main content in a scrollable container with `flex-1 overflow-y-auto` and use `scrollbar-thin` class
   ```tsx
   <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-6 scrollbar-thin">
     {/* Your form or content here */}
   </div>
   ```

4. **Fixed Footer**: Make `DialogFooter` fixed with `flex-shrink-0`, custom padding, and border-top for visual separation
   ```tsx
   <DialogFooter className="flex-shrink-0 px-6 pb-6 pt-4 border-t">
   ```

### Width Guidelines

- **Mobile**: Use `max-w-[calc(100%-2rem)]` to ensure proper margins on small screens
- **Desktop**: Use responsive classes like `sm:max-w-4xl`, `sm:max-w-2xl`, etc. based on content needs
- **Important**: Always use responsive prefixes (e.g., `sm:max-w-4xl`) to override the default `sm:max-w-md` from `DialogContent`

### Height Guidelines

- Use `max-h-[90vh]` on `DialogContent` to prevent modals from exceeding viewport height
- The flexbox layout ensures proper scrolling behavior

### Scrolling Best Practices

- **Never** apply `overflow-y-auto` directly to `DialogContent` - it creates ugly scrollbars
- **Always** create a separate scrollable container for the content area
- Use the `scrollbar-thin` utility class for clean, subtle scrollbars
- The scrollbar styling is already defined in `globals.css` and supports both light and dark modes

### Complete Modal Template

```tsx
<Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={handleOpenChange}>
  <DialogContent className="max-w-[calc(100%-2rem)] sm:max-w-4xl max-h-[90vh] flex flex-col p-0">
    <DialogHeader className="flex-shrink-0 px-6 pt-6 pb-4">
      <DialogTitle>Modal Title</DialogTitle>
      <DialogDescription>Modal description</DialogDescription>
    </DialogHeader>
    <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-6 scrollbar-thin">
      {/* Main content - forms, lists, etc. */}
    </div>
    <DialogFooter className="flex-shrink-0 px-6 pb-6 pt-4 border-t">
      {/* Action buttons */}
    </DialogFooter>
  </DialogContent>
</Dialog>
```

### Key Points

- ✅ Header and footer stay fixed while content scrolls
- ✅ Clean, thin scrollbar that doesn't break the modal's beauty
- ✅ Proper responsive width handling
- ✅ Consistent padding and spacing
- ✅ Visual separation with border-top on footer
- ❌ Never put overflow on DialogContent directly
- ❌ Never use non-responsive max-width classes (they get overridden)

## Form & Validation Implementation Standards

When creating forms with validation, always use the shadcn `Form` components with `react-hook-form` and `zod` for validation.

### Required Dependencies

```typescript
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod'
import {
  Form,
  FormControl,
  FormField,
  FormItem,
  FormLabel,
  FormMessage,
  FormDescription,
} from '@/shared/ui/form'
import { Input } from '@/shared/ui/input'
import { Textarea } from '@/shared/ui/textarea'
```

### Form Setup Pattern

1. **Define Zod Schema**: Create validation schema in `@/lib/validators.ts`
   ```typescript
   export const createFormSchema = z.object({
     title: z.string().min(1, "Title is required"),
     description: z.string().optional(),
   })
   
   export type CreateFormData = z.infer<typeof createFormSchema>
   ```

2. **Initialize Form**: Use `useForm` with `zodResolver`
   ```typescript
   const form = useForm<FormData>({
     resolver: zodResolver(formSchema),
     defaultValues: {
       title: '',
       description: '',
     },
   })
   
   const { handleSubmit, control } = form
   ```

3. **Wrap with Form Provider**: Always wrap form with `Form` component
   ```tsx
   <Form {...form}>
     <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
       {/* Form fields */}
     </form>
   </Form>
   ```

### FormField Pattern

**Always use `FormField` with `control` prop** - never use `register` directly:

```tsx
// ✅ Good: Using FormField with control
<FormField
  control={control}
  name="title"
  render={({ field }) => (
    <FormItem>
      <FormLabel>Title</FormLabel>
      <FormControl>
        <Input placeholder="Enter title" {...field} />
      </FormControl>
      <FormMessage />
    </FormItem>
  )}
/>

// ❌ Bad: Using register directly
<Input {...register('title')} />
```

### FormField Structure

Every `FormField` should follow this structure:

```tsx
<FormField
  control={control}
  name="fieldName"
  render={({ field }) => (
    <FormItem>
      <FormLabel>
        Label Text
        {/* Optional required indicator */}
        <span className="text-destructive">*</span>
      </FormLabel>
      <FormControl>
        {/* Input, Textarea, Select, or custom component */}
        <Input {...field} />
      </FormControl>
      {/* Optional description */}
      <FormDescription>
        Helper text or description
      </FormDescription>
      {/* Error message (automatically shown) */}
      <FormMessage />
    </FormItem>
  )}
/>
```

### Custom Components in Forms

When using custom components (like `CategorySelector`), integrate them with `FormField`:

```tsx
<FormField
  control={control}
  name="categoryId"
  render={({ field }) => (
    <FormItem>
      <FormLabel>Category</FormLabel>
      <FormControl>
        <CategorySelector
          value={field.value || ''}
          onChange={(value) => field.onChange(value || '')}
          placeholder="Select category"
        />
      </FormControl>
      <FormMessage />
    </FormItem>
  )}
/>
```

### Form Submission Pattern

```typescript
const onSubmit = async (data: FormData) => {
  setIsSubmitting(true)
  try {
    await submitHandler(data)
    // Handle success (close modal, show toast, etc.)
  } catch (error) {
    // Error handling is automatic via FormMessage
    console.error('Form submission error:', error)
  } finally {
    setIsSubmitting(false)
  }
}
```

### Form State Management

- **Loading State**: Use `isSubmitting` state for submit button
  ```tsx
  <Button type="submit" disabled={isSubmitting}>
    {isSubmitting ? 'Saving...' : 'Save'}
  </Button>
  ```

- **Default Values**: Set in `useForm` config or via `useEffect` for edit forms
  ```typescript
  const defaultValues = isEditing && data ? {
    title: data.title,
    description: data.description || '',
  } : {
    title: '',
    description: '',
  }
  
  const form = useForm({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema),
    defaultValues,
  })
  ```

### Complete Form Example

```tsx
'use client'

import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod'
import { Form, FormControl, FormField, FormItem, FormLabel, FormMessage } from '@/shared/ui/form'
import { Input } from '@/shared/ui/input'
import { Button } from '@/shared/ui/button'
import { formSchema, type FormData } from '@/lib/validators'

export function MyForm({ onSubmit, isSubmitting }: MyFormProps) {
  const form = useForm<FormData>({
    resolver: zodResolver(formSchema),
    defaultValues: {
      title: '',
      description: '',
    },
  })

  const { handleSubmit, control } = form

  return (
    <Form {...form}>
      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} className="space-y-6">
        <FormField
          control={control}
          name="title"
          render={({ field }) => (
            <FormItem>
              <FormLabel>
                Title <span className="text-destructive">*</span>
              </FormLabel>
              <FormControl>
                <Input placeholder="Enter title" {...field} />
              </FormControl>
              <FormMessage />
            </FormItem>
          )}
        />

        <FormField
          control={control}
          name="description"
          render={({ field }) => (
            <FormItem>
              <FormLabel>Description</FormLabel>
              <FormControl>
                <Textarea placeholder="Enter description" {...field} />
              </FormControl>
              <FormMessage />
            </FormItem>
          )}
        />

        <Button type="submit" disabled={isSubmitting}>
          {isSubmitting ? 'Saving...' : 'Save'}
        </Button>
      </form>
    </Form>
  )
}
```

### Key Points

- ✅ Always use `Form`, `FormField`, `FormControl`, `FormItem`, `FormLabel`, `FormMessage`
- ✅ Always use `control` prop with `FormField`, never `register` directly
- ✅ Define schemas in `@/lib/validators.ts` with Zod
- ✅ Export TypeScript types from schema using `z.infer<typeof schema>`
- ✅ Use `FormDescription` for helper text
- ✅ Handle loading states with `isSubmitting`
- ✅ Set proper `defaultValues` in `useForm` config
- ❌ Never use `register` directly - always use `FormField` with `control`
- ❌ Never use custom `FormInput` component - use shadcn `Input` with `FormField`
- ❌ Never manually display errors - use `FormMessage` component
- ❌ Never skip validation schema - always use Zod schemas

### Migration Checklist

When refactoring existing forms:
- [ ] Replace `register` with `FormField` and `control`
- [ ] Replace `FormInput` with shadcn `Input` inside `FormControl`
- [ ] Replace manual error display with `FormMessage`
- [ ] Wrap form with `Form` provider
- [ ] Ensure Zod schema exists in `@/lib/validators.ts`
- [ ] Export TypeScript type from schema
- [ ] Use `FormDescription` for helper text instead of separate `<p>` tags

